1
00:00:54,955 --> 00:00:57,015
[Phone Clicks]

2
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:03,562
You. Give me that.

3
00:01:03,638 --> 00:01:06,370
What are you talking about?

4
00:01:06,443 --> 00:01:08,811
- Mrs. Miller?
- Shh. Your phone, young lady.

5
00:01:08,881 --> 00:01:11,943
- I was just switching it to silent
in case someone called.
- No, you were cheating.

6
00:01:12,020 --> 00:01:14,285
- Please. Like I have to cheat-
- Lady?

7
00:01:14,358 --> 00:01:16,657
I said wait!

8
00:01:16,729 --> 00:01:19,700
Give it here.
[Sighs]
Sure.

9
00:01:24,177 --> 00:01:26,807
Love the blouse.

10
00:01:29,787 --> 00:01:34,386
- L-I need to use the bunk room.
- The bathroom? Sure.

11
00:01:34,462 --> 00:01:37,262
- In 19 minutes.
- Can't do it.

12
00:01:37,334 --> 00:01:39,964
You got a friend in the bathroom
with the answers?

13
00:01:40,039 --> 00:01:44,206
[Grunting, Groaning]

14
00:01:44,281 --> 00:01:47,776
Fine. I'll get you an escort.

15
00:01:54,800 --> 00:01:57,828
Don't stink up till I kill you.

16
00:01:59,209 --> 00:02:00,905
Don't stand up.

17
00:02:00,978 --> 00:02:04,974
[Panting]
D.X.D.T.

18
00:02:05,053 --> 00:02:07,181
Hey, he's really sick.

19
00:02:11,665 --> 00:02:13,497
- Eyes on your work.
- Are you all right?

20
00:02:15,005 --> 00:02:17,270
- What is going on?
- Get some help.

21
00:02:54,378 --> 00:02:56,472
[Foreman]
The kid was just taking
his A.P. Calculus exam,

22
00:02:56,549 --> 00:02:58,677
when all of a sudden
he got nauseous and disoriented.

23
00:02:58,753 --> 00:03:01,121
That's the way calculus presents.

24
00:03:01,191 --> 00:03:04,219
Severe bradycardia. Heart rate's
down to 48 and falling fast.

25
00:03:04,296 --> 00:03:06,732
You know the kid?
No, he just came in.

26
00:03:06,801 --> 00:03:10,900
Mom real good looking?
I... didn't notice.

27
00:03:10,976 --> 00:03:13,412
Then it's a mystery.

28
00:03:13,480 --> 00:03:15,814
Not why he's sick,
but why you care so much.

29
00:03:15,884 --> 00:03:18,821
Gift shop's open. Buy him a card.
He's not responding to atropine.

30
00:03:18,890 --> 00:03:22,829
Boys love fart jokes.
Find one with a good fart joke.

31
00:03:22,897 --> 00:03:26,802
He's a teenager. It's drugs.

32
00:03:26,872 --> 00:03:31,813
Tell those E.R. Geniuses to give him charcoal
and naloxone. Stop following me.

33
00:03:31,881 --> 00:03:35,581
His tox screen was negative.
He's still whacked out.

34
00:03:35,655 --> 00:03:40,118
You don't care about the kid.
You just find his illness intriguing.

35
00:03:40,196 --> 00:03:43,326
His CAT scan was clean.
There's no sign of infection.
It's not diabetes.

36
00:03:43,403 --> 00:03:45,702
And you don't care about him.

37
00:03:45,774 --> 00:03:48,540
If that's what you wanna hear,
not in the slightest.

38
00:03:48,613 --> 00:03:53,178
Me neither.
May as well be objective.

39
00:03:58,899 --> 00:04:01,061
Maybe it's shigellosis.

40
00:04:01,136 --> 00:04:02,798
Wouldn't account
for the bradycardia.

41
00:04:02,872 --> 00:04:04,807
Viral myocarditis?

42
00:04:04,876 --> 00:04:06,811
Wouldn't account for the whacked out.

43
00:04:06,880 --> 00:04:10,249
- So, what's the differential
for a negative tox screen?
- He was clean.

44
00:04:10,320 --> 00:04:13,450
- Unless someone screwed up the test.
- That never happens.

45
00:04:13,525 --> 00:04:17,988
Or he O.D.'d on something
we didn't test for. 1,4-Butanediol
would give you these symptoms.

46
00:04:18,067 --> 00:04:22,006
- That's a new one.
- Copy machine toner.

47
00:04:22,075 --> 00:04:27,176
Same punch as G.H.B.
A little pricier. Way more dangerous.

48
00:04:27,251 --> 00:04:31,588
On the other hand, it is legal.
I want you to go to his house
and find his stash.

49
00:04:31,659 --> 00:04:34,027
I'll bet you know
all the good hiding spots.

50
00:04:34,097 --> 00:04:36,032
[Sighs]
Actually, I never did drugs.

51
00:04:36,101 --> 00:04:39,072
Better go with him.
In case he gets high.

52
00:04:41,512 --> 00:04:44,312
- How much atropine is the kid getting?
- One m.I.

53
00:04:44,383 --> 00:04:47,081
Make it two. In case he needs
his heart later on.

54
00:04:49,092 --> 00:04:51,961
[Chase]
Matt, did you take
any drugs this morning?

55
00:04:52,031 --> 00:04:54,627
Pencils down.

56
00:04:54,702 --> 00:04:57,969
Can you squeeze
my fingers for me?

57
00:05:01,182 --> 00:05:04,642
Did anyone give you anything
to help with the math test?

58
00:05:06,357 --> 00:05:09,624
- Matt?
- I told you. He doesn't take drugs.

59
00:05:09,697 --> 00:05:13,602
Ms. Davis, all parents
think that about their children,

60
00:05:13,671 --> 00:05:15,867
and they're usually wrong.

61
00:05:15,942 --> 00:05:18,936
I gave my mum a little trouble
when I was his age.

62
00:05:19,014 --> 00:05:21,279
I turned out okay.
Even she thought so.

63
00:05:21,352 --> 00:05:25,348
I know my kid.
The fact that you did drugs
doesn't mean he did.

64
00:05:27,698 --> 00:05:29,690
Did he go anywhere last night?

65
00:05:31,972 --> 00:05:34,374
No, he was home studying.

66
00:05:34,443 --> 00:05:37,312
- Any friends come over to help him?
- And bring him drugs?

67
00:05:37,382 --> 00:05:39,578
Is that what your friends did?
Ms. Davis.

68
00:05:39,653 --> 00:05:44,356
Look, I tested him myself.

69
00:05:44,428 --> 00:05:46,990
The home kit.
I took some hair off his brush.

70
00:05:47,066 --> 00:05:50,504
I didn't want him to know about it,
because I do trust him.

71
00:05:50,573 --> 00:05:52,508
Then why did you test him?

72
00:05:55,349 --> 00:06:00,086
All he did last night was study
and argue about his college interviews.

73
00:06:00,158 --> 00:06:04,461
He wants to be himself-
no haircut, ratty old clothes.

74
00:06:04,532 --> 00:06:06,467
That'll go over big.

75
00:06:06,536 --> 00:06:09,063
Then we both gave up
and went to bed. That's it.

76
00:06:09,141 --> 00:06:13,478
Has he ever had any problems
like this before? Any family history?

77
00:06:13,549 --> 00:06:16,748
- [Erratic Beeping]
- No, it's just me and Matt.

78
00:06:16,822 --> 00:06:18,814
Diazepam. Ten milligrams, stat.
[Nurse]
Right.

79
00:06:18,893 --> 00:06:22,228
- What's going on?
- Just taking some precautions.

80
00:06:22,299 --> 00:06:27,002
He's seizing.
Help me hold him.

81
00:06:27,075 --> 00:06:31,014
Stay cool, mate.
We'll calm you down.

82
00:06:31,082 --> 00:06:34,646
[Beeping Continues]

83
00:06:41,167 --> 00:06:43,831
This room is way too clean
for a teenage boy.

84
00:06:43,906 --> 00:06:47,173
- You know why House thinks I'm a druggie?
- This is gonna be a racial thing, isn't it?

85
00:06:47,245 --> 00:06:52,312
Same reason he thinks this kid overdosed.
When you're a drug addict,
you wanna believe everyone else is too.

86
00:06:52,389 --> 00:06:55,485
He's not addicted.
He has to take drugs.

87
00:06:55,561 --> 00:06:57,997
The definition of an addict.

88
00:06:58,066 --> 00:07:00,969
He's in pain.
And addicted to pain killers.

89
00:07:01,038 --> 00:07:03,668
What a coincidence!

90
00:07:05,413 --> 00:07:08,043
You really never did any drugs?

91
00:07:08,118 --> 00:07:10,314
[Laughs]
Now this is gonna be a racial thing.

92
00:07:10,389 --> 00:07:15,023
[Laughs]
Deflecting a personal question with a joke.
Gee, who do I know that does that?

93
00:07:15,098 --> 00:07:20,096
Yeah, I'm just like him.
Except for the angry, bitter,
pompous cripple part.

94
00:07:20,174 --> 00:07:22,804
Maybe we should all pitch in
and get you a nice cane.

95
00:07:22,878 --> 00:07:25,747
You already have
the matching gym shoes.

96
00:07:34,600 --> 00:07:37,366
Check this out!

97
00:07:37,439 --> 00:07:40,308
Mom's not too careful
with the homemade tomato sauce.

98
00:07:43,083 --> 00:07:46,054
When the top sticks out like that,
it's a sign of bacterial contamination.

99
00:07:52,968 --> 00:07:54,903
This one's open.

100
00:08:01,049 --> 00:08:03,383
[Machine Beeps]

101
00:08:03,454 --> 00:08:06,289
I am extremely disappointed in you.

102
00:08:07,929 --> 00:08:11,367
I send you out for exciting
new designer drugs,

103
00:08:11,435 --> 00:08:14,235
and you come back
with tomato sauce.

104
00:08:14,308 --> 00:08:17,872
I bet you paid twice as much.
I got mine online.

105
00:08:17,948 --> 00:08:22,217
Matt decided to make himself
a homemade pizza for a bedtime snack.

106
00:08:22,290 --> 00:08:24,726
Source of botulism,
as well as a million other toxins...

107
00:08:24,794 --> 00:08:27,594
that cause gastroenteritis,
cardiac symptoms and mental confusion.

108
00:08:27,666 --> 00:08:31,969
I'm not so sure about gastroenteritis.
But mental confusion? Bring it on.

109
00:08:32,041 --> 00:08:36,344
- Don't- But-
- Mmm. Delicious.

110
00:08:36,416 --> 00:08:39,216
- Do you have a death wish?
- I notice you didn't try to save me.

111
00:08:39,288 --> 00:08:42,384
Lfigured you were trying to make
some kind of subtle point.

112
00:08:42,460 --> 00:08:46,194
I was. The kid just started seizing.
Not a symptom of food-borne toxins.

113
00:08:46,268 --> 00:08:49,763
Also not a symptom of drug use.
Not two hours after admission.

114
00:08:49,841 --> 00:08:54,612
So what would make him seize,
in addition to all these other
delightful symptoms?

115
00:08:54,683 --> 00:08:57,381
- Pesticide poisoning.
- Carbamates.

116
00:08:57,455 --> 00:09:00,051
Or organophosphates,
organochlorines.

117
00:09:00,127 --> 00:09:03,725
- Inhalation or absorption?
- Too soon to tell.

118
00:09:03,800 --> 00:09:06,396
We should wash him down.
The poison could still be on his skin.

119
00:09:06,472 --> 00:09:09,443
Already told the nurses.

120
00:09:22,902 --> 00:09:25,634
[Woman On P.A.]
Nursing supervisor to the instrument room.

121
00:09:25,708 --> 00:09:29,477
- I feel good.
- That's your complaint?

122
00:09:30,984 --> 00:09:34,114
Your major symptom.
I told her this was a waste of time.

123
00:09:34,190 --> 00:09:36,091
I notice colors more.

124
00:09:36,161 --> 00:09:40,692
And music! L-
I'm really hearing music.

125
00:09:40,769 --> 00:09:46,542
I'm 82, and I'm supposed to be playing
canasta with the other old ladies,

126
00:09:46,613 --> 00:09:51,782
but now... when I see a guy
with a cute butt,

127
00:09:51,856 --> 00:09:55,157
I can't stop looking at him.

128
00:09:55,229 --> 00:09:58,998
Or... a sexy beard.

129
00:09:59,070 --> 00:10:04,410
And you figure that enjoying
cute butts is a sign of disease?

130
00:10:04,480 --> 00:10:08,852
It all started a month ago when Mark
rented Gone With the Wind for me,

131
00:10:08,921 --> 00:10:11,585
but it had the wrong DVD in it.

132
00:10:11,660 --> 00:10:13,526
Oh! Oh, right. L-I forgot.
This is all my fault.

133
00:10:13,597 --> 00:10:17,092
Of course, he was
too busy to bring it back.
Yeah, like I don't have a life.

134
00:10:17,170 --> 00:10:21,007
So I watched it,
and it had this actor in it-

135
00:10:21,078 --> 00:10:24,015
this kid called Ashton Kutcher.

136
00:10:24,083 --> 00:10:28,284
Now... I think about Ashton...

137
00:10:28,358 --> 00:10:31,352
all the time.

138
00:10:31,431 --> 00:10:34,995
All... the time.

139
00:10:35,070 --> 00:10:39,407
- Ah.
- You remind me of him.

140
00:10:39,479 --> 00:10:42,143
Same bedroom eyes.

141
00:10:42,217 --> 00:10:44,653
People are always mixing us up.

142
00:10:44,721 --> 00:10:47,920
L-l-I suppose you-
you need to check my heart?

143
00:10:47,994 --> 00:10:52,058
- No! No, no, that-
that won't be necessary.
- I told you.

144
00:10:52,136 --> 00:10:54,504
But I am going to admit you
to the hospital for tests.

145
00:10:54,574 --> 00:10:58,513
What tests? She's just old.
And you're just insufferable.

146
00:10:58,581 --> 00:11:01,643
Your mother has had a sudden personality
change. It should be checked out.

147
00:11:01,720 --> 00:11:05,659
I'll have a nurse come in and admit her.
I'm too handsome to do paperwork.

148
00:11:05,727 --> 00:11:08,926
[Door Closes]
[Giggles]

149
00:11:09,000 --> 00:11:11,596
[Beeping]

150
00:11:11,672 --> 00:11:14,507
[Woman On P.A.]
Anesthesiologist to O.R. Room 4.

151
00:11:14,577 --> 00:11:17,480
Anesthesiologist to O.R. Room 4.

152
00:11:19,787 --> 00:11:23,988
[Sighs]
He's been on this medication
for over an hour.

153
00:11:24,061 --> 00:11:28,296
Pralidoxime is very effective.
It just takes a little time for it to kick in.

154
00:11:28,370 --> 00:11:31,341
And maybe you're wrong.
Maybe it's not a pesticide.

155
00:11:31,409 --> 00:11:34,869
The blood work was pretty conclusive.
It's an organophosphate.

156
00:11:34,949 --> 00:11:38,718
[Erratic Beeping]

157
00:11:38,789 --> 00:11:40,815
Oh, my God.

158
00:11:40,893 --> 00:11:44,023
Move back.
Ms. Davis, move back, please.

159
00:11:45,835 --> 00:11:48,362
We're at 30. Bradycarciac arrest.
Get me the pads.

160
00:11:48,440 --> 00:11:51,377
- [Beeping Continues]
- What are you doing?

161
00:11:51,446 --> 00:11:56,217
We're gonna beat his heart for him.
Set it at 60.

162
00:11:56,288 --> 00:11:59,350
[Beeping Continues]

163
00:12:05,138 --> 00:12:08,268
[Steady Beeping]

164
00:12:22,671 --> 00:12:25,403
[Sighs]

165
00:12:27,480 --> 00:12:31,044
[Monitor Continues Beeping]
I can't stand this anymore.

166
00:12:31,120 --> 00:12:34,057
We'll keep him on the pads
for another hour.
Then what?

167
00:12:35,294 --> 00:12:37,229
Let's see what happens.

168
00:12:39,268 --> 00:12:42,034
Well, that's not my philosophy.

169
00:12:42,107 --> 00:12:44,406
Especially when it comes to my son.

170
00:12:44,478 --> 00:12:49,818
And if something bad might happen,
I'd like to know what our options are.

171
00:12:51,992 --> 00:12:54,986
The pralidoxime isn't doing him any good.
We're gonna have to wire his heart.

172
00:12:55,065 --> 00:12:57,797
- Maybe we're wrong about the pesticides.
- I ran his plasma again, twice.

173
00:12:57,870 --> 00:13:00,272
Are there any stronger treatments
for the organophosphate poisoning?

174
00:13:00,341 --> 00:13:05,146
Oh, damn it! You caught me.
We went with the weak stuff.
I was just trying to save a little money.

175
00:13:05,217 --> 00:13:08,518
Actually, one of my professors
at Columbia developed an experimental
treatment for the army.

176
00:13:08,590 --> 00:13:12,050
- What's the success rate?
- They're targeted. There's a different
hydrolase treatment for each poison.

177
00:13:12,130 --> 00:13:14,828
We'd need to know if Matt got hit
with Orthene or malathion-

178
00:13:14,901 --> 00:13:18,168
- What's the success rate?
- Excellent, I'm sure. It's the U.S. Army.

179
00:13:18,241 --> 00:13:22,146
"Be all you can be." Point is,
what are the kid's chances without it?

180
00:13:22,216 --> 00:13:25,153
Minimal at best. The poison's
broken the blood-brain barrier.

181
00:13:25,221 --> 00:13:29,456
I assume "minimal at best"
is your stiff-upper-lip British way
of saying "no chance in hell."

182
00:13:29,529 --> 00:13:33,992
- I'm Australian.
- You put the queen on your money.
You're British. Call your professor.

183
00:13:34,071 --> 00:13:36,871
If we don't know what kind
of organophosphate we're targeting,

184
00:13:36,942 --> 00:13:39,310
we don't know
which hydrolase to ask for.

185
00:13:39,381 --> 00:13:42,876
- There's over 40 organophosphates.
- I'll get all of'em.
- Get all of'em.

186
00:13:42,954 --> 00:13:47,189
- Great minds think alike.
- By the time they get here, we're
gonna know which one we need.

187
00:13:47,262 --> 00:13:50,700
If we figure out how he got exposed,
we'll figure out what he was exposed to.

188
00:13:50,768 --> 00:13:52,703
Well, the mom had
homemade tomato sauce.

189
00:13:52,772 --> 00:13:55,265
Call me crazy, but I'm thinking
maybe... homemade tomatoes?

190
00:13:55,343 --> 00:13:57,505
Front yard. Vegetable garden.

191
00:13:57,581 --> 00:14:00,017
Kid's out there spraying,
sees a pretty girl,

192
00:14:00,085 --> 00:14:03,181
hormones raging,
spray can goes off in his face.

193
00:14:03,258 --> 00:14:06,788
- I'll check into it.
- I'll make the call.

194
00:14:06,865 --> 00:14:09,859
I'll keep the kid alive.
For a while, at least.

195
00:14:11,073 --> 00:14:13,269
I'll have lunch.

196
00:14:13,344 --> 00:14:15,643
Through the superior vena cava...

197
00:14:16,817 --> 00:14:19,082
into the right atrium...

198
00:14:19,155 --> 00:14:21,887
through the tricuspid valve...

199
00:14:23,663 --> 00:14:27,466
and lodging to the wall
of the right ventricle.

200
00:14:29,708 --> 00:14:32,577
Can you turn off the pad?

201
00:14:32,647 --> 00:14:36,950
[Instruments Beeping]

202
00:14:37,021 --> 00:14:40,790
- Capturing?
- Getting a native heart rate of 36.

203
00:14:40,862 --> 00:14:42,922
No, that won't do.
Let's get him to 50.

204
00:14:43,000 --> 00:14:46,130
[Instruments Beeping]

205
00:15:06,977 --> 00:15:10,814
"The healer with his magic powers.!

206
00:15:10,885 --> 00:15:15,416
"I could rub his gentle brow for hours.

207
00:15:15,493 --> 00:15:20,264
"His manly chest, his stubbled jaw.

208
00:15:20,336 --> 00:15:22,898
Everything about him leaves me raw"-

209
00:15:22,974 --> 00:15:26,309
- Psych ward's upstairs.
- " With joy.

210
00:15:26,380 --> 00:15:32,255
Oh, House, your very name
will never leave this girl the same."

211
00:15:32,324 --> 00:15:36,320
It's not bad for an 82-year-old.
She asked me to give that
to her true love.

212
00:15:36,399 --> 00:15:39,268
What can I say?
Chicks with no teeth turn me on.

213
00:15:39,337 --> 00:15:42,331
That's fairly disgusting.
And that's ageism.

214
00:15:42,410 --> 00:15:44,880
You better watch yourself
around this babe.

215
00:15:44,948 --> 00:15:47,441
Here are her test results.

216
00:15:52,195 --> 00:15:55,064
Impressive.
[Phone Ringing]

217
00:15:56,102 --> 00:15:58,572
[Phone Beeps]
Yeah.

218
00:15:58,640 --> 00:16:00,802
I found the pesticide.
It's disulfoton.

219
00:16:00,878 --> 00:16:04,111
And it's empty.
He used up the whole can.
Okay, I'll get Chase on it.

220
00:16:04,184 --> 00:16:09,251
The poison's called disulfoton.
This should bond with it
and neutralize the poison.

221
00:16:09,326 --> 00:16:12,992
Then his nervous system
will start sending the correct
signals to the rest of his body.

222
00:16:13,068 --> 00:16:16,471
But there was no disul-
well, that stuff in that can.

223
00:16:16,541 --> 00:16:18,772
The label says "disulfoton."
I can show you.

224
00:16:18,845 --> 00:16:20,814
But you didn't test it.

225
00:16:20,881 --> 00:16:23,681
You said Matt sprayed with it
this morning before school.

226
00:16:23,754 --> 00:16:26,520
Matt started that garden himself
for an environmental science class.

227
00:16:26,592 --> 00:16:29,119
- They weren't allowed to use pesticides.
- Apparently he cheated a little.

228
00:16:29,198 --> 00:16:33,901
It was orange peel oil. Totally organic.
He dumped that other stuff last winter.

229
00:16:33,973 --> 00:16:37,342
The symptoms fit. There must
have been some left in the can.

230
00:16:37,413 --> 00:16:40,316
If you're wrong, what will
this treatment do to him?

231
00:16:40,384 --> 00:16:44,517
Well, theoretically, it could
increase the toxicity and-

232
00:16:44,593 --> 00:16:46,687
But we're not wrong.
Don't.

233
00:16:46,763 --> 00:16:50,223
We're not wrong.
I can't let you do this.

234
00:16:54,645 --> 00:16:58,846
Get a court order.
Unless you wanna see someone
killed by sheer irrationality.

235
00:16:58,919 --> 00:17:02,449
Maternal instinct is always irrational.
Doesn't mean it's wrong.

236
00:17:02,527 --> 00:17:04,895
Actually, that's the definition.

237
00:17:04,964 --> 00:17:07,024
It doesn't make her
mentally incompetent.

238
00:17:07,101 --> 00:17:09,036
She's risking her son's life...

239
00:17:09,105 --> 00:17:11,940
based on a teenager's claim
that he washed something.

240
00:17:12,011 --> 00:17:14,105
How mentally incompetent
can you get?

241
00:17:14,182 --> 00:17:16,516
That's a brilliant legal argument.

242
00:17:18,622 --> 00:17:23,221
Listen, have your Harvard law dog
whip up some high-grade
legal-sounding psychobabble:

243
00:17:23,298 --> 00:17:26,463
"Temporary insanity brought on
by acute panic distress syndrome."

244
00:17:26,537 --> 00:17:28,802
I don't care. We have to give him
the hydrolase.

245
00:17:28,875 --> 00:17:32,210
Her only sign of mental illness
is that she disagrees with you.

246
00:17:32,281 --> 00:17:34,774
Some would consider that
a sign of sanity.

247
00:17:34,853 --> 00:17:39,088
Not the kid. Let's ask him.
Oh, no, I forgot. We can't. He's dying.

248
00:17:39,161 --> 00:17:44,467
Get the mother to sign off
that she's refusing this treatment.

249
00:17:54,155 --> 00:17:57,217
Good-bye.
[Clears Throat]

250
00:17:57,295 --> 00:18:01,234
"I, Margo Davis,
have been informed of the risks...

251
00:18:01,302 --> 00:18:03,898
"which may arise from my refusal
of advised medical care.

252
00:18:03,974 --> 00:18:06,570
- I hereby release"-
- Who are you?

253
00:18:06,646 --> 00:18:08,808
I work for the hospital.

254
00:18:08,883 --> 00:18:11,877
'"...the Princeton Plainsboro
Teaching Hospital and its employees,

255
00:18:11,956 --> 00:18:16,157
"agents and otherwise, for any adverse
medical conditions resulting from my refusal.

256
00:18:16,230 --> 00:18:19,258
It is not the hospital's fault
if my son kicks off."

257
00:18:19,336 --> 00:18:24,835
- Kicks off?
- I punched up the language.
Mostly for clarification.

258
00:18:24,913 --> 00:18:28,750
"I understand my doctors consider
my decision to be completely idiotic"-

259
00:18:28,820 --> 00:18:32,258
Why are you doing this?
"But I am convinced
I know more than they do.

260
00:18:32,327 --> 00:18:34,889
I took a biology course in high school."

261
00:18:34,965 --> 00:18:37,230
I assume that's-Yeah.

262
00:18:37,303 --> 00:18:42,040
"Besides, I enjoy controlling
every single aspect of my son's life,

263
00:18:42,112 --> 00:18:44,947
even if it means his death."

264
00:18:46,152 --> 00:18:49,351
Sign here, please.
I brought a pen.

265
00:18:51,296 --> 00:18:54,894
Who are you?
I'm the doctor
who's trying to save your son.

266
00:18:54,969 --> 00:18:57,735
You're the mom who's letting him die.

267
00:18:57,807 --> 00:19:00,437
Clarification- It's a beautiful thing.

268
00:19:06,657 --> 00:19:11,325
[Man On P.A.]
Dr. Morell, contact the CAPlab.
Dr. Morell, contact the CAPlab.

269
00:19:11,400 --> 00:19:15,772
She rethought her position.
Start him on the hydrolase.

270
00:19:15,841 --> 00:19:18,277
No. We've got a problem.

271
00:19:20,049 --> 00:19:21,950
Three. One, two, three.

272
00:19:23,623 --> 00:19:26,423
Heart rate's 49.
O2 stat, 84 and falling.

273
00:19:26,494 --> 00:19:28,896
Saline, atropine and diazepam, now.

274
00:19:28,965 --> 00:19:32,027
His name is Chi Ling.
He was admitted 12 minutes ago.

275
00:19:32,105 --> 00:19:35,304
Identical symptoms as Matt.
Chase isn't sure he's gonna make it.

276
00:19:45,464 --> 00:19:48,492
This one. Bag.

277
00:19:58,688 --> 00:20:01,056
[Beeping]

278
00:20:03,831 --> 00:20:06,734
Matt's next-door neighbor,
by any chance?

279
00:20:06,803 --> 00:20:09,171
They live 10 miles apart.

280
00:20:09,241 --> 00:20:11,677
Apparently, they
don't even know each other.

281
00:20:16,622 --> 00:20:19,525
[Heart Monitor Beeping]

282
00:20:33,854 --> 00:20:37,292
We live in an apartment.
We have two bamboo trees.

283
00:20:37,360 --> 00:20:40,992
No pesticides.
Just dry seaweed for fertilizer.
[Sobbing]

284
00:20:41,067 --> 00:20:45,404
The second kid's heart
is almost as weak as Matt's.
We may need to get it wired up too.

285
00:20:45,475 --> 00:20:49,414
And I don't know how long
Matt's brain will hold up. His heart's
not pumping enough oxygen.

286
00:20:49,482 --> 00:20:51,417
How much are you giving him?
80%.

287
00:20:51,486 --> 00:20:53,682
Make it 90.
We're risking damage
to his retina.

288
00:20:53,757 --> 00:20:56,387
What the heck. Do it anyway.
He's a calculus student.

289
00:20:56,462 --> 00:20:59,922
Probably likes having a brain.
I'll have Foreman take care of it.

290
00:21:00,002 --> 00:21:03,600
- I'm the primary.
- Yeah, but the mom
wants to get rid of you.

291
00:21:03,675 --> 00:21:05,871
And you know me.
I'm a people-pleasing kind of guy.

292
00:21:05,946 --> 00:21:09,441
She complained? About me?
She went to the principal.

293
00:21:09,519 --> 00:21:13,959
Told Cuddy you took drugs in high school.
This is gonna go on your permanent record.

294
00:21:14,028 --> 00:21:17,158
I was trying to get information.
It was a strategy.

295
00:21:17,234 --> 00:21:19,260
She also used the word "slacker."
You wanna come in?

296
00:21:19,338 --> 00:21:23,072
Smoke a little weed?
Watch some MTV?

297
00:21:24,548 --> 00:21:26,984
High school has no idea
what could have happened.

298
00:21:27,053 --> 00:21:30,024
You sure that Chi didn't sneak
onto Matt's tomato patch?

299
00:21:30,091 --> 00:21:32,287
Maybe they sniffed disulfoton
together to get high.

300
00:21:32,362 --> 00:21:34,593
Same school, different grades,
different cliques, different everything.

301
00:21:34,667 --> 00:21:37,331
They managed to get poisoned together.
They must have something in common.

302
00:21:37,406 --> 00:21:41,937
Their classes aren't even
in the same building. They don't even
eat lunch in the same lunchroom.

303
00:21:42,014 --> 00:21:44,176
How do they get to school?

304
00:21:46,088 --> 00:21:50,391
Unless you're with the board,
the police or the union,
I don't care who you are.

305
00:21:50,463 --> 00:21:52,398
Sir, we need to test the bus
for chemical residue.

306
00:21:52,467 --> 00:21:54,629
And I need to pick up
the basketball team.

307
00:21:54,705 --> 00:21:57,039
You got a problem with that,
call my supervisor.

308
00:21:57,109 --> 00:22:00,171
Two kids were poisoned
on your bus this morning.

309
00:22:00,247 --> 00:22:02,581
They're dying.

310
00:22:06,893 --> 00:22:09,921
My God. The things he said.

311
00:22:09,999 --> 00:22:13,597
[Sighs]
Dr. House wanted your son
to get the medicine he needed.

312
00:22:13,673 --> 00:22:17,578
- He was willing to do whatever
it took to make that happen.
- The wrong kind of medicine.

313
00:22:17,647 --> 00:22:21,848
[Machine Beeps]
He didn't know that.
At the time, it was our only choice.

314
00:22:21,921 --> 00:22:24,083
He would have known
if he had listened to me.

315
00:22:24,159 --> 00:22:26,595
[Sighs]
He listened.

316
00:22:26,663 --> 00:22:29,361
He just assumed you were wrong.

317
00:22:31,205 --> 00:22:34,108
To be honest, that's true
of most of our patients.

318
00:22:34,178 --> 00:22:37,742
[Scoffs]
You're just as pompous
and superior as he is!

319
00:22:40,991 --> 00:22:44,896
Uh, Matt is back row right.
The Asian kid, uh, first or second row left.

320
00:22:44,964 --> 00:22:48,835
Okay.
Look, I got this rash
in kind of my groin region.

321
00:22:48,906 --> 00:22:51,843
It's, um, not a symptom.

322
00:22:51,911 --> 00:22:54,677
Neither is being obnoxious.
Looks like you're in the clear.

323
00:23:00,861 --> 00:23:04,493
- So who poisoned 'em?
- We're not sure yet
if it was done on purpose.

324
00:23:04,568 --> 00:23:08,701
Oh, yeah? You oughta see these little
bastards screaming and punching
each other all day long.

325
00:23:08,776 --> 00:23:12,579
Did you happen to notice
if anyone was doing any spraying
near the bus route?

326
00:23:12,649 --> 00:23:16,918
Oh! Yeah, there was this truck
down by the pond doing
something or other.

327
00:23:16,991 --> 00:23:19,826
It smelled kinda funny too.

328
00:23:26,141 --> 00:23:30,308
- Oh! Dr. House!
- Well, it took you long enough.
We've been waiting here-

329
00:23:30,383 --> 00:23:32,910
Mr. Adams, would you
step outside for a moment?

330
00:23:32,988 --> 00:23:36,255
Why?
Because you irritate me.

331
00:23:36,327 --> 00:23:38,729
He is my son, Doctor.

332
00:23:38,798 --> 00:23:41,428
He's just a little cranky
from not eating.

333
00:23:43,574 --> 00:23:48,105
Fine. Mrs. Adams, have you had
any recent sexual activity?

334
00:23:48,182 --> 00:23:50,117
What?

335
00:23:50,186 --> 00:23:54,285
Well, l- I don't suppose
fantasies count.

336
00:23:54,360 --> 00:23:56,124
Oh, my God.

337
00:23:56,197 --> 00:23:59,727
Unfortunately,
I've hit kind of a dry spell.

338
00:23:59,804 --> 00:24:03,573
Only for the last-
oh, I don't know- 15 years.

339
00:24:03,644 --> 00:24:08,278
You see? She's- She's confused.
My father died eight years ago.
Heart attack.

340
00:24:08,353 --> 00:24:10,584
Not in bed, dear.

341
00:24:13,129 --> 00:24:15,964
Why do you ask?

342
00:24:16,034 --> 00:24:20,440
Because you have tested
positive for syphilis.

343
00:24:20,509 --> 00:24:24,505
That's impossible!
And insulting.

344
00:24:26,320 --> 00:24:30,259
I don't think it's the first time
that Mom has heard this diagnosis.

345
00:24:33,400 --> 00:24:37,999
"Cupid's disease."
That's what we used to call it.

346
00:24:38,075 --> 00:24:40,773
When did you get it?

347
00:24:40,848 --> 00:24:46,154
Uh, 1939. Prom night, I think.

348
00:24:46,224 --> 00:24:48,626
He had a Chevrolet.

349
00:24:49,730 --> 00:24:51,824
It was before I met your father.

350
00:24:51,901 --> 00:24:54,463
But you said that-

351
00:24:54,539 --> 00:24:57,874
You said Dad was your first love.

352
00:24:57,945 --> 00:25:00,107
He was.

353
00:25:00,183 --> 00:25:04,680
We're talking about sex.

354
00:25:06,262 --> 00:25:08,254
But I was treated.

355
00:25:08,331 --> 00:25:11,496
Which suppressed it-
in your case, for 60 years.

356
00:25:11,571 --> 00:25:17,070
But now it's back, and
the spirochetes that cause syphilis
are eating away at your brain cells.

357
00:25:17,148 --> 00:25:19,778
Oh! That's revolting.

358
00:25:19,853 --> 00:25:23,120
It's not as revolting as chlamydia.
That's got seepage.

359
00:25:23,193 --> 00:25:25,458
And it's the 21 st century.

360
00:25:25,530 --> 00:25:29,025
We've got flying cars,
robot dogs and penicillin.

361
00:25:29,104 --> 00:25:31,631
High-dose regimen
for two weeks, you're cured.

362
00:25:31,709 --> 00:25:34,145
- I can't believe this.
- Yeah, well, pinch yourself.

363
00:25:34,214 --> 00:25:39,212
I guess for the next two weeks,
I'd better practice safe sex.

364
00:25:41,393 --> 00:25:45,992
You'll be fine.
Just feed that cranky kid.

365
00:25:51,379 --> 00:25:56,320
The county's worried about West Nile,
so they sprayed ethyl-parathion
right next to the bus route.

366
00:25:56,388 --> 00:25:59,018
Do you have the hydrolase
for ethyl-parathion?

367
00:25:59,093 --> 00:26:01,996
Yeah. Only one problem. Matt's mother
faxed his records to the C.D.C.

368
00:26:02,065 --> 00:26:05,799
She refuses to let us do anything
until she hears back from them.

369
00:26:05,872 --> 00:26:08,775
Well, you're getting good
at this Godlike doctor racket.

370
00:26:08,845 --> 00:26:10,973
Why don't you talk her
out of this lunacy?

371
00:26:11,049 --> 00:26:13,177
She's not really
a big fan of mine.
Or mine.

372
00:26:13,253 --> 00:26:18,194
Well, only one man left in the bull pen,
and he throws like a girl.

373
00:26:20,133 --> 00:26:22,626
I hate sports metaphors.

374
00:26:23,773 --> 00:26:25,969
Give her the high, hard one.

375
00:26:26,044 --> 00:26:28,708
We really think
the hydrolase is the only-

376
00:26:28,782 --> 00:26:30,910
I'm thinking about transferring
Matt out of here.

377
00:26:30,986 --> 00:26:34,116
Ms. Davis, your son is very sick.
He won't survive a transfer.

378
00:26:34,192 --> 00:26:37,322
I'm getting a second opinion
from the Center for Disease Control.

379
00:26:37,398 --> 00:26:41,167
Right. Look,
when my grandmother got sick-

380
00:26:41,239 --> 00:26:43,766
What? You're gonna tell me
some tough decision you had to make?

381
00:26:43,843 --> 00:26:48,283
- I know how hard this is for you.
- Maybe embroider the story
a little? Make it fit?

382
00:26:48,352 --> 00:26:50,845
I can't imagine being in your position.

383
00:26:50,923 --> 00:26:54,555
Honesty. A kind of vulnerable honesty.
That's your thing. And the looks.

384
00:26:54,630 --> 00:26:58,933
- Oh, boy.
- They send a single woman
to hustle the single mom.

385
00:26:59,005 --> 00:27:01,441
Actually, they sent a doctor!

386
00:27:01,509 --> 00:27:04,036
To tell you that if your son
doesn't get this treatment,

387
00:27:04,115 --> 00:27:06,677
there's a good chance he'll die.

388
00:27:06,753 --> 00:27:10,453
That's what House said
about a treatment
that would have killed Matt.

389
00:27:10,526 --> 00:27:13,656
You've gotta
do better than that.
No, it's on you.

390
00:27:13,732 --> 00:27:16,931
You need to do better.
Right now. Yes or no?

391
00:27:19,810 --> 00:27:22,679
- I don't know.
- Figure it out.

392
00:27:23,751 --> 00:27:25,686
Wait.

393
00:27:27,491 --> 00:27:30,986
[Heart Monitor Beeping]

394
00:28:19,121 --> 00:28:21,717
[Alarm Beeping]
Nurse!

395
00:28:21,793 --> 00:28:24,855
- Help!
- Okay. Coming.

396
00:28:24,932 --> 00:28:28,632
- Seizing. Suction.
- [Woman] Watch his head.

397
00:28:28,705 --> 00:28:31,198
My son!

398
00:28:33,381 --> 00:28:35,475
[Alarm Continues Beeping]

399
00:28:35,552 --> 00:28:38,352
- [Woman] Coming.
- Diazepam.

400
00:28:38,423 --> 00:28:40,449
Got it.

401
00:28:42,331 --> 00:28:44,596
- Suction.
- Ten milligrams.

402
00:28:44,669 --> 00:28:47,504
- Right.
- He's not responding.
Another 10 milligrams.

403
00:28:47,574 --> 00:28:49,509
You're killing him!

404
00:28:51,180 --> 00:28:54,481
- You're killing him.!
- Get her out of here. Get her outta here!

405
00:28:54,553 --> 00:28:59,118
- Come on, ma'am. Ma'am?
- [Erratic Beeping]

406
00:29:05,274 --> 00:29:08,109
[Foreman]
Their hearts are barely pumping.
Their lungs are shot.

407
00:29:08,179 --> 00:29:10,478
Now they're showing liver toxicity.

408
00:29:10,550 --> 00:29:13,885
[House]
I guess Matt's mom won't nominate us
for any Doctor of the Year awards.

409
00:29:13,956 --> 00:29:17,416
Only explanation is
they got poisoned by something
besides ethyl-parathion.

410
00:29:17,496 --> 00:29:20,763
Then the hydrolase
would release the neurotoxins
instead of bonding with them.

411
00:29:20,836 --> 00:29:22,998
The only thing they have
in common is the bus.

412
00:29:23,073 --> 00:29:25,008
Except their symptoms.
Given their severity...

413
00:29:25,077 --> 00:29:29,517
and the fact their cardiac symptoms
are getting worse much faster than their
other ones, what does that tell us?

414
00:29:29,586 --> 00:29:31,920
The poison was probably
absorbed through the skin.

415
00:29:31,990 --> 00:29:34,961
They were admitted at 8:45 and 11:00.
When were they poisoned?

416
00:29:35,029 --> 00:29:37,898
Absorption through the skin?
Anywhere from three to eight hours.

417
00:29:37,968 --> 00:29:40,495
In a case that bad, more like
one and a half to four.

418
00:29:40,573 --> 00:29:44,535
So Matt was poisoned before 7:15.
Before he got on the bus.

419
00:29:44,614 --> 00:29:49,248
You're thinking each kid
got poisoned in his own house?
Two separate exposures?

420
00:29:49,323 --> 00:29:52,590
What do teenage boys do
in the morning, besides the obvious?

421
00:29:52,662 --> 00:29:55,360
Wake up, go to the bathroom,

422
00:29:55,434 --> 00:29:58,530
then the obvious,
then check their zits in the mirror.

423
00:29:58,607 --> 00:30:02,478
- Do they both use the same acne cream?
- Because acne cream contains lanolin,

424
00:30:02,547 --> 00:30:05,780
which could have pesticides,
and you're thinking they both
bought the same product-

425
00:30:05,853 --> 00:30:09,519
- It was contaminated. Shaving cream.
- Chi doesn't look like he shaves.

426
00:30:09,593 --> 00:30:11,755
But he sweats.
Deodorant could contain lanolin.

427
00:30:11,831 --> 00:30:14,893
Do these people have animals?
Chi has a dog. Matt has a cat.

428
00:30:14,970 --> 00:30:18,066
[Cameron]
Fleas. Flea powder.
Okay. Go to their houses.

429
00:30:18,143 --> 00:30:19,975
Check for anything that
might have touched their skin...

430
00:30:20,046 --> 00:30:22,242
between the time they got up
and the time they went to school.

431
00:30:22,317 --> 00:30:24,616
I'm running low on tomato sauce.

432
00:30:24,688 --> 00:30:29,891
[Panting]
Oh! Dr. House! Hello.

433
00:30:29,965 --> 00:30:34,166
- I sent you home.
- Well, I came back.

434
00:30:34,240 --> 00:30:37,234
I took a cab,

435
00:30:37,311 --> 00:30:41,114
so my son wouldn't try
to chaperone us this time.

436
00:30:41,186 --> 00:30:44,487
I'm sorry, but... the fact
that the sexual pleasure center...

437
00:30:44,559 --> 00:30:47,428
of your cerebral cortex
has been overstimulated by spirochetes is-

438
00:30:47,497 --> 00:30:52,529
It's a poor basis for a relationship.
I learned that one the hard way.

439
00:30:53,676 --> 00:30:58,207
Doctor, um, these feelings
that I've been having-

440
00:30:58,285 --> 00:31:00,983
Is it all because of the syphilis?

441
00:31:01,056 --> 00:31:02,319
Yes.

442
00:31:04,429 --> 00:31:09,097
Then... here's the prescription
you gave me.

443
00:31:15,082 --> 00:31:18,680
Well, it's not likely
I'm going to infect anyone.

444
00:31:18,756 --> 00:31:21,591
No, but it'll kill you.

445
00:31:22,897 --> 00:31:24,991
Well, gotta go sometime,

446
00:31:25,068 --> 00:31:27,264
and...
[Sighs]

447
00:31:27,339 --> 00:31:32,076
I really don't want to play
canasta for the rest of my life.

448
00:31:32,148 --> 00:31:36,247
L- I like feeling sexy again...

449
00:31:36,322 --> 00:31:41,923
and... making a fool out of myself
with handsome, young doctors.

450
00:31:41,999 --> 00:31:44,834
[Laughs]

451
00:31:44,905 --> 00:31:48,343
Do you think that
I would have given you this...

452
00:31:48,411 --> 00:31:51,211
if it would stop you
from flirting with me?

453
00:31:51,283 --> 00:31:55,313
Well, but if I'm cured-
Then all the spirochetes will die off.

454
00:31:55,391 --> 00:32:00,890
But the little pieces of your cerebral cortex
that have been destroyed won't grow back.

455
00:32:02,304 --> 00:32:05,139
You're brain damaged.

456
00:32:05,210 --> 00:32:08,147
Doomed to feeling good
for the rest of your life.

457
00:32:08,215 --> 00:32:10,446
[Laughing]

458
00:32:10,519 --> 00:32:14,253
Really? Well, thank you.

459
00:32:14,326 --> 00:32:16,192
Georgia.

460
00:32:16,263 --> 00:32:19,200
Yeah.
[Sighs]

461
00:32:19,269 --> 00:32:22,206
When I stop being contagious,

462
00:32:22,274 --> 00:32:25,211
I'll come by for a checkup.

463
00:32:30,122 --> 00:32:32,957
[Sighs]
Yeah.

464
00:32:36,768 --> 00:32:39,067
- Matt uses Sure.
- Chi uses Old Spice.

465
00:32:39,139 --> 00:32:41,575
- No zit cream.
- Lucky kid.

466
00:32:41,644 --> 00:32:44,513
Floral air freshener.
Doesn't matter.

467
00:32:44,583 --> 00:32:48,522
When was the last time you heard
of a teenage boy using air freshener?

468
00:32:48,590 --> 00:32:50,525
Pert shampoo.

469
00:32:50,594 --> 00:32:52,688
Um, Johnson's over here.

470
00:32:53,934 --> 00:32:56,028
Matt's mom would make him
use air freshener.

471
00:32:56,104 --> 00:32:58,768
There are limits
to a mother's power. Even hers.

472
00:32:58,842 --> 00:33:00,743
Just check.

473
00:33:02,449 --> 00:33:04,611
Negative on the floral.

474
00:33:04,687 --> 00:33:06,622
Matt's A.L.T.'s are up to 800.

475
00:33:06,691 --> 00:33:09,958
If they get any higher,
we can toss his liver.
[Phone Ringing]

476
00:33:10,030 --> 00:33:13,058
- Yeah.
- No matches on flea powder
or underarm deodorant.

477
00:33:13,135 --> 00:33:15,765
- Or any other kind of deodorant.
- What about shampoo?

478
00:33:15,841 --> 00:33:19,678
No to shampoo, bar soap,
dish soap, dish detergent.

479
00:33:19,748 --> 00:33:22,742
How about laundry detergent?
Maybe they both washed
their clothes this morning.

480
00:33:22,821 --> 00:33:24,949
I'll check.
[Foreman]
You know, if we cure Matt,

481
00:33:25,025 --> 00:33:28,190
he can use his close brush with death
on his college essays.

482
00:33:28,263 --> 00:33:30,995
- Admission guys love that stuff.
- That's how you got in, right?

483
00:33:31,069 --> 00:33:33,265
Jailhouse diary?

484
00:33:34,376 --> 00:33:37,472
Uh, TKO.

485
00:33:37,548 --> 00:33:42,147
TKO. Liquid?

486
00:33:42,224 --> 00:33:44,455
Liquid, 128 ounces.

487
00:33:44,528 --> 00:33:47,988
- 128. Yellow jug. "Special"-
- "Environmentally safe formula."

488
00:33:48,068 --> 00:33:50,162
It's the same.
Bring in the detergent.

489
00:33:50,239 --> 00:33:52,937
So the detergent was
contaminated with pesticides?

490
00:33:53,010 --> 00:33:56,004
Which soaked into their clothes
and got absorbed into their skin.

491
00:33:57,719 --> 00:34:01,385
Hi. Uh, listen, the clothes
your son wore today-

492
00:34:01,459 --> 00:34:04,294
- He washed them this morning, right?
- What?

493
00:34:04,365 --> 00:34:07,803
Nice, respectful Asian kid
does the laundry.

494
00:34:07,871 --> 00:34:11,206
[Speaking Chinese]
My wife said he didn't.

495
00:34:13,014 --> 00:34:16,383
Well, then I'm betting he washed them
last night without you knowing.

496
00:34:16,454 --> 00:34:20,757
[Speaking Chinese]
He doesn't even know
how to turn on the machine.

497
00:34:20,829 --> 00:34:24,825
And those clothes were new.
Nobody washed them.
His clothes are all new?

498
00:34:24,903 --> 00:34:28,033
The ones he wore today, yes.
Never washed.

499
00:34:36,190 --> 00:34:38,125
Now what?

500
00:34:40,565 --> 00:34:43,502
What about Matt's clothes?
Were they new?

501
00:34:43,571 --> 00:34:46,633
Uh, they're ratty old jeans,
I think.

502
00:34:46,710 --> 00:34:48,645
They've been bagged
and taken downstairs.

503
00:34:58,900 --> 00:35:01,370
"Davis."
Yeah, they're Matt's.

504
00:35:04,744 --> 00:35:06,679
And they're old.

505
00:35:06,748 --> 00:35:10,448
And yet the label
isn't faded in the slightest.

506
00:35:10,521 --> 00:35:14,187
Fake old. Hundred dollars
for the homeless look.

507
00:35:14,261 --> 00:35:17,791
What? Kids aren't
pretentious in England?

508
00:35:18,870 --> 00:35:22,171
Chi was wearing a different brand.
How could they both
have been contaminated?

509
00:35:22,243 --> 00:35:26,114
That is the question for the ages.
Meantime, get these tested.

510
00:35:49,127 --> 00:35:52,064
[Beeping]

511
00:35:56,040 --> 00:35:58,977
I never bother to wash
new clothes before I wear them.

512
00:35:59,045 --> 00:36:03,178
Right. Your wife does. She likes you
feeling nice and comfortable.

513
00:36:03,253 --> 00:36:06,418
Hey, I'm a man.
I don't have time for laundry.

514
00:36:06,493 --> 00:36:09,020
I'm saving lives here.

515
00:36:09,097 --> 00:36:11,590
No, she doesn't wash them either.

516
00:36:11,669 --> 00:36:16,268
So your skin absorbs a little dye,
the odd chemical here and there.

517
00:36:16,344 --> 00:36:18,746
You know me-
Mr. Living On The Edge.

518
00:36:18,815 --> 00:36:22,275
Judging by the self-importance
of your strut, you have identified
the chemical in question.

519
00:36:22,355 --> 00:36:25,485
- Phosdrin.
- Hit 'em with the hydrolase.

520
00:36:25,561 --> 00:36:29,159
- Chi's parents said yes, but-
- Mighty Mom said no.

521
00:36:29,234 --> 00:36:34,175
Well, she's gonna feel like a million bucks
when Chi lives and her son dies.

522
00:36:34,244 --> 00:36:38,912
Send Cameron. She's the only one of you
who's managed to talk her into anything.

523
00:36:38,986 --> 00:36:42,584
Not this time. Matt's mom
won't make a move until she gets
that opinion from the C.D.C.

524
00:36:44,429 --> 00:36:47,093
[Scoffs]
Godot would be faster.

525
00:37:13,918 --> 00:37:15,853
[Slams Mug Down]

526
00:37:20,664 --> 00:37:22,599
What is this?

527
00:37:22,668 --> 00:37:25,104
Thought I'd hang out.

528
00:37:27,343 --> 00:37:31,214
- In case you change your mind.
- [TV: Soap Opera]

529
00:37:31,284 --> 00:37:33,549
Then I could give Matt
the medicine right away.

530
00:37:38,430 --> 00:37:42,062
[Sighs]
Don't worry. I've got time.

531
00:37:44,642 --> 00:37:47,044
The C.D.C. Promised they'd call.

532
00:37:47,113 --> 00:37:49,048
I'm sure they will.

533
00:37:49,116 --> 00:37:52,383
Maybe tomorrow.
Maybe the day after.

534
00:37:52,456 --> 00:37:55,291
It takes time
to review faxed records.

535
00:37:55,362 --> 00:38:00,065
Then they'll probably tell you
they can't make a decision
based on faxed records.

536
00:38:02,242 --> 00:38:04,541
This is Suzie.

537
00:38:04,613 --> 00:38:07,243
She's never gonna marry him.
No money.

538
00:38:07,317 --> 00:38:09,479
What makes you think
you're right this time?

539
00:38:10,824 --> 00:38:13,488
Same reason as last time.

540
00:38:16,835 --> 00:38:20,365
If you wanna see how the other kid
does first, that's okay.

541
00:38:20,442 --> 00:38:24,006
Might help you. Although
there's a real good chance-

542
00:38:24,082 --> 00:38:29,353
- What? Matt might kick off?
- That's a little blunt.

543
00:38:29,425 --> 00:38:31,553
I was gonna say "run out of time"...

544
00:38:31,629 --> 00:38:35,693
or just kind of let my voice trail off.

545
00:38:37,708 --> 00:38:41,772
[Cell Phone Ringing]

546
00:38:45,021 --> 00:38:48,960
Hello?
Yes, this is Margo Davis.

547
00:38:53,370 --> 00:38:56,068
But you have his records.
I sent them.

548
00:38:59,949 --> 00:39:02,111
Yes, I understand.

549
00:39:04,123 --> 00:39:06,058
[Phone Beeps]

550
00:39:09,467 --> 00:39:12,927
They can't help me.
They haven't seen him.

551
00:39:13,007 --> 00:39:16,308
They can't make a decision-
Give it to him.

552
00:39:27,801 --> 00:39:31,763
[Southern Accent]
"The C.D.C. Is unable to give
an opinion at this time, ma'am.

553
00:39:31,841 --> 00:39:35,336
We're gonna have a doctor
in your area next week."

554
00:39:35,416 --> 00:39:37,909
You fooled her with that?

555
00:40:45,712 --> 00:40:49,378
- [Beeping]
- House!

556
00:41:29,427 --> 00:41:33,457
So I'm gonna be cured?
As long as you don't wear
any more poison pants.

557
00:41:35,205 --> 00:41:38,370
I'm sorry, Mom.
I knew the pants were stolen.

558
00:41:38,444 --> 00:41:42,747
They were only five bucks.
You're gonna give me hell,
aren't you?

559
00:41:42,819 --> 00:41:45,688
Oh, honey,
I'm just glad you're alive.

560
00:41:45,758 --> 00:41:48,057
Let's get you home
so you can rest.

561
00:41:48,130 --> 00:41:50,726
Maybe I am too hard on you.

562
00:41:50,801 --> 00:41:52,997
You don't have to make up
that test till next week.

563
00:41:53,072 --> 00:41:56,772
You can stay in bed
and work on your applications.

564
00:41:56,845 --> 00:41:59,941
Some guy was selling pants
off the back ofhis truck near the school.

565
00:42:00,018 --> 00:42:02,511
When he wasn't busy
as a clothing entrepreneur,

566
00:42:02,589 --> 00:42:05,025
he did day labor
at a cornfield out on Route 1.

567
00:42:05,094 --> 00:42:07,496
He used the same truck
for both jobs.

568
00:42:07,565 --> 00:42:11,129
So he spilled pesticide on the pants
and was too lazy to clean up.

569
00:42:11,206 --> 00:42:14,405
Real question is
why are you still here?

570
00:42:14,478 --> 00:42:17,847
Why aren't you out there
making up with the joyful mother?

571
00:42:17,918 --> 00:42:21,584
[Sighs]
Why would I do that?
No reason at all.

572
00:42:21,659 --> 00:42:25,325
If you don't care about her.
Or her son.

573
00:42:31,143 --> 00:42:34,137
Oh, Dr. House.

574
00:42:34,215 --> 00:42:36,241
The C.D.C. Called.

575
00:42:37,388 --> 00:42:40,188
- Again.
- Oops.

576
00:42:43,500 --> 00:42:47,530
- Who are those guys?
- Oh, they're the arrogant jerks
that saved your life.

577
00:42:54,186 --> 00:42:56,121
[Elevator Bell Dings]

578
00:43:08,746 --> 00:43:10,772
[Elevator Bell Dings]

579
00:43:46,783 --> 00:43:48,046
That's some bad hat, Harry.

9999
00:00:0,500 --> 00:00:2,00
<font color="#ffff00" size=14>www.tvsubtitles.net</font>
